== A Wife’s Submission to Christ First

=== §. 51. #emph[Of cases in which a wife ought not to refrain from what her husband forbids.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 4, §. 26.]

So much for the distinct branches of a wife’s subjection. The #emph[limitation] and #emph[manner] of performing it follow next.

To know the #emph[limitation] of a wife’s obedience, and the #emph[manner] in which she ought to yield subjection to her husband, two things must be considered:

+ The place of a husband.
+ The place of a wife.

The husband’s place is noted in this phrase, #emph[as to the Lord:] #footnote[Eph 5:22.] by which it is shown that the husband, even by virtue of his place, stands to his wife in Christ’s place. This is laid down still more plainly in the words that follow, “the husband is #footnote[#emph[v.] 23.] the head of the wife, as Christ is the head of the church.”

The wife’s place is indicated in these words, “as the church #footnote[#emph[v.] 24.] is subject to Christ, so let wives be to their own husbands.” From these it is clear that the obedience which a wife performs to her husband must be such an obedience as the church performs to Christ.

From the place of a husband I gather this general ground concerning a wife’s subjection, that

#emph[Subjection must be yielded to the husband as to Christ.] From this two conclusions will follow. One is #emph[negative,] which is this:

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[The wife must yield no other subjection to her husband than what is consistent with her subjection to Christ.]]

The other is affirmative, which is this:

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[The wife must subject herself to her husband in the manner in which she would or should subject herself to Christ.]]

The former is a necessary condition required of all inferiors in their subjection and obedience (as I showed #footnote[Treatise 1, §. 6.] before), and much more in a wife’s subjection to her husband, because of all who are unequal #footnote[See §. 4. of this Treatise.] there is the least disparity between husbands and wives.

From this, for our present purpose, I gather these two other, more particular conclusions. The first of them is this:

1\. #emph[If God expressly commands the wife some duty, and her husband will not by any means consent that she should do it, but forbids her, she may and ought to do it without his consent, or against it.]

Two cautions are to be carefully observed about this conclusion: #footnote[2\. Cautions limiting a wife’s persisting in doing what her husband forbids.]

1\. That the wife be sure that God has commanded her what she does without or against her husband’s consent. If she is in doubt, then she must wait, and refrain until she gains his consent. When two opposite cases meet together, and the one is doubtful, the other plain and express, the doubtful case must give way to the more evident. Now the law of subjection is unqualified: “your desire will be subject to your husband.” #footnote[Gen 3:16.] Its extent is general, #emph[in everything.] The only reservation and exception is #emph[in the Lord.] Therefore, if the wife is not sure that what her husband forbids her is #emph[against the Lord,] she must refrain from doing it.

The second caution is that she use all the good means she can to gain her husband’s consent before she does, against his consent, even what is commanded. In this way she will testify to her subjection both to God and to her husband. To God, in that nothing can keep her from doing his express commandment: she will rather offend her husband than God, when one of them must be offended. To her husband, in that she pushes it to the utmost, and uses all the means she can to avoid offending him, so much so that he himself might see (if the god of this world did not blind his eyes) that the offense is in no way given on her part, but merely taken on his.

For proof of this, it is true beyond all contradiction that the wife is not bound to greater subjection to her husband than the subject is to the magistrate. But a subject ought not to refrain from a binding duty commanded by God because his governor forbids him. Take the example of Daniel, #footnote[Dan 6:7, #emph[etc.]] who prayed to God daily, though the king had made a solemn decree that no one should make any petition to God or man for thirty days except to the king. Take also the apostles, #footnote[Acts 4:18, #emph[etc.]] who preached the gospel, though they were expressly forbidden.

Though the Scripture is plentiful in giving examples of wives’ subjection, yet it is very sparing in recording examples of those who refused to be subject in such justifiable cases, so that wives would not take too great a liberty from them.

Some are recorded, but they are either extraordinary or not in every way justifiable. Abigail’s example#footnote[1 Sam 25:18.] was extraordinary, and therefore not to be imitated except in similar extraordinary cases.

The example of Rebekah,#footnote[Gen 27:6.] which may seem somewhat more to the point, is not in every way to be justified. For though what she intended was in substance very good, and ought to have been done, namely the #emph[blessing of Jacob] (for God foretold that the blessing belonged to Jacob when he said, “The older will serve the younger”),#footnote[Gen 25:23.] yet because she did not remind her husband of God’s word, nor labor to persuade him to fulfill it, but went about the matter deceitfully, she cannot be justified in it. But in general this example shows that God’s word must be yielded to rather than a husband’s will.

For a better application of this point, I will lay down some particular instances agreeable to God’s word. Suppose a wife well instructed in the true religion is married to an idolatrous or profane husband, and he, without any just cause, forbids her to go to church, especially on the Lord’s Day, to pray in English, to read the word, to teach her children the principles of religion, to restore what she has gotten unjustly and fraudulently, and the like: she may and must do these things all the same.

#emph[Objection.] Why should the giving of alms not be counted among these?

#emph[Answer.] 1. Because the husband has a greater power over the goods than over these things.

2\. Because almsgiving is not absolutely commanded to all, but to those who have the means to give; but these things are absolutely commanded to all.

=== §. 52. #emph[Of cases in which a wife ought to refrain from what her husband requires.]

The other particular conclusion is this, that

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[If a husband requires his wife to do what God has forbidden, she ought not to do it.]]

Two cautions like the former are likewise to be observed #footnote[2\. Cautions limiting a wife’s refusing to do what her husband commands.] about this point.

First, that she be sure (being truly informed by God’s word) that what she refuses to do at her husband’s command is forbidden by God.

Secondly, that she first labor with all meekness, and by all the good means she can, to dissuade her husband from urging and pressing on her what she cannot do with a good conscience.

A like proof may be brought for this as was brought for the former. For we know that a wife is not bound to greater subjection to her husband than a son is to a father; but a son may, in the case put forward, refrain from doing what his father requires and commands him to do. Take the approved example of Jonathan, who refused to bring David #footnote[1 Sam 20:31.] to Saul to be killed, though his father commanded him to do so. I might also show the same in Saul’s subjects and #footnote[1 Sam 22:17.] servants, who refused to kill the priests of the Lord at his command. Though a husband is not named in particular among those to whom we are forbidden to listen if they #footnote[Deut 13:6.] entice us to idolatry, yet by the rule of relation he is implied, and is gathered by just consequence from this clause, “your friend who is as your own soul”; for who is so dear as a husband?

To illustrate this in some particulars, as I did the former: if a husband commands his wife to go to Mass, to a stage play, to play at dice, to prostitute her body to impurity, to go about garishly and whorishly dressed, to sell by scant weights, short measures, or the like, she ought not to do it.

=== §. 53. #emph[Of wives’ faults in showing more respect to their husbands than to God.]

Contrary to this limitation is, on the one side, a fawning, flattering disposition in those wives who seek to please their husbands so much that they do not care if they displease God (Jezebel was such a one; to please her husband she most wickedly #footnote[1 Kgs 21:7. #emph[etc.]] plotted Naboth’s death); and on the other side, a fainting, timid heart, which makes them fear their husbands more than they fear God. Good Sarah,#footnote[Gen 12:13. #emph[etc.]] that worthy example of good wives in other things, failed somewhat in this. If wives duly considered, and always remembered, that they have a husband (namely Christ) in heaven as well as on earth, and that there is a greater difference between #emph[that] husband and #emph[this] than between heaven and earth, and that in giving reward and in taking revenge there is no comparison between them, then their care to please their husband in heaven, or their fear of offending him, would be much greater than their care to please, or fear to offend, their husband on earth. If anything were commanded or forbidden them by their husbands on earth against Christ, they would say, “If I do this, or refrain from that, I would be dealing falsely against my own soul; for nothing can be hidden from my husband in heaven. Yes, in this I would be obeying Satan rather than God.”

=== §. 54. #emph[Of the manner of a wife’s subjection to her husband.]

The second general conclusion, concerning the manner of a wife’s subjection, which was gathered from the place of a husband, was this, that

#emph[The wife must subject herself to her husband in the manner in which she would or should subject herself to Christ.] The particle #emph[As] in this clause #emph[(as to the Lord)] implies as much.

This very conclusion is also inferred from the #emph[place] of a wife. In the same place that the church is to Christ, a wife is to a husband; therefore such subjection as the church yields to Christ, a wife must yield to her husband. The very words of the apostle expressly affirm this.#footnote[Eph 5:24.] Now we know that every Christian wife in her own person ought to yield that obedience to Christ which the church in general does; therefore she also must yield such subjection to her husband as she should to Christ.

#emph[Question.] #footnote[See §. 5. of this Treatise.] What if a husband is an enemy of Christ? Must such subjection be yielded to an enemy of Christ as to Christ himself?

#emph[Answer.] Yes, because in his office he stands in Christ’s place, though in his heart he is an enemy. In this case the wisdom, patience and obedience of a wife will be best tested. It is said of the church that she is a lily among thorns.#footnote[Song 2:2.] She remains lily-like, white, soft, pleasant and lovely, though she is joined with thorns, which are rough, prickly and sharp. So a wife must be mild, meek, gentle and obedient, though she is married to a crooked, perverse, profane, wicked husband. In this way her virtue and grace will shine out the more clearly, just as the stars shine most brightly in the darkest night. Among wives Abigail deserves great praise, who did not forget her duty, though she was married to a churlish, covetous, drunken sot, a very Nabal in name and deed. As for those who take occasion from the wickedness of their husbands to neglect their duty, they add a curse to their cross. For it is a cross to have a bad husband, but to be a bad wife is a sin, which brings down a curse. Therefore let wives turn their eyes from the disposition of their husband’s person to the condition of his place; and, by virtue of that place, seeing that he bears Christ’s image, #emph[be subject to him as to Christ.]

This general conclusion might be applied to the #emph[matter] of subjection as well as to the #emph[manner.] For the church acknowledges Christ as her superior, fears him inwardly, reverences him outwardly, and obeys him both by refraining from what he forbids and by doing what he commands. Since these points have been distinctly and fully handled before, and applied to wives, I will not repeat them again. Therefore, to dwell now on the manner only, there are four #footnote[Four graces needed to season a wife’s subjection.] virtues which are especially needed for this, with which the church seasons her subjection to Christ, and with which wives also may and must season their subjection to their husbands.

These are the four:

1\. Humility, 2. Sincerity, 3. Cheerfulness, 4. Constancy.

=== §. 55. #emph[Of wives’ humility in every duty.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 4, §. 15.]

Humility is that grace which keeps one from thinking of himself more highly than is fitting, and, because of the lowly opinion he has of himself, makes him think reverently and highly of others. So if humility is placed in a wife’s heart, it will make her think better of her husband than of herself, and so make her the more willing to yield him all subjection. The apostle requires it of all #footnote[Phil 2:3. Eph 4:2.] Christians as a general sauce to season all other duties; but in a special way it is needed by inferiors, and most of all by wives, because there are #footnote[See before §. 4.] many privileges belonging to their place, which may soon make them think they ought not to be subject, unless they are humbly minded. That the church seasons her subjection with it is clear from the Song of Songs, where she often acknowledges her own lowliness and the excellence of her spouse.

#emph[Therefore as the church is humbly subject to Christ, so let wives be to their husbands.]

=== §. 56. #emph[Of wives’ pride.]

Contrary to this is pride, which puffs wives up and makes them think there is no reason why they should be subject to husbands: they can rule themselves well enough, yes, and rule their husbands too, as well as their husbands rule them. There is no more pestilent vice in an inferior than this. It is the cause of all rebellion, disobedience and disloyalty: “Strife comes only from pride.” #footnote[Prov 13:10.]

=== §. 57. #emph[Of wives’ sincerity in every duty.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 4, §. 63.]

#smallcaps[II]. Sincerity is that grace which makes one #emph[inwardly,] in #emph[truth,] what he appears #emph[outwardly] to be #emph[in show.] This is that “sincerity of heart”#footnote[Eph 6:5.] which is expressly required of servants, and it may be applied to wives, for indeed it belongs to all kinds of people. Because it is discerned only by the Lord, who “knows everyone’s heart,”#footnote[Acts 1:24.] it will move a wife to keep her eye on him in all she does, and to endeavor to approve herself to him above all. That is why uprightness and walking before God are often joined together: he who is upright will surely walk before God, that is, endeavor to approve himself to God, as Noah#footnote[Gen 6:9.] did, and as #footnote[Gen 17:1.] God commanded Abraham to do.

Though there were no other motive in the world to move her to subjection, yet for the sake of conscience toward Christ she should yield it. Peter#footnote[1 Pet 3:5.] testifies of holy women that they “put their hope in God and were subject to their husbands,” implying by this that their conscience toward God made them subject to their husbands. Was not Sarah’s subjection seasoned with sincerity when #emph[within herself,]#footnote[Gen 18:12.] in her heart, she called her husband #emph[lord?]

There is great reason why wives should subject #footnote[Reasons for sincerity.] themselves in sincerity. For

1\. In their subjection even to their husbands they have to #footnote[1\. Because wives have to deal with Christ.] deal with Christ, in whose place their husbands stand. So, though their husbands, who are but men, see only the face and outward behavior, yet Christ sees their heart and inward disposition. Though their husbands see only the things they do before their faces, and can hear only of such things as are done before others, yet Christ sees and knows the things that are done in the most secret places there can be, when no creature besides themselves knows of it. Now let it be granted that in their outward conduct they give their husbands very good satisfaction and please them in every way; yet if sincerity has been lacking, with what face can they appear before Christ? He will take a very different kind of account of them. Before Christ all their outward show will be of no use to them at all.

2\. In this lies a main difference between true, Christian, #footnote[2\. This makes a difference between holy women and others.] religious wives and merely natural women. #emph[These] may be subject for self-interested reasons: namely, so that their husbands may love them the more, or live the more quietly and peaceably with them; or so that they may the more readily obtain what they desire from their husbands; or for fear of their husband’s displeasure and wrath, knowing him to be an angry, furious man, so that otherwise it might go worse with them: they might lack many needful things, or receive many hard blows, if they were not subject. But #emph[the other] have regard to Christ’s ordinance, by which their husbands are made their head, and to his word and will, by which subjection is commanded them. In this way #footnote[1 Pet 3:5.] holy women subjected themselves. They cannot be holy who do not subject themselves in this way; for this is a sweet perfume that sends up a pleasing fragrance into Christ’s nostrils, and makes the things we do pleasant and acceptable to him.

3\. The benefit of this virtue, once planted in a wife’s heart, #footnote[3\. Great is the benefit of it.] is very great, both to her husband and to herself.

To her husband, in that it will make her show her respect for him before others, behind his back, as well as before himself in his presence. It will also make her faithful to him, and careful to do his will wherever he is, whether with her or away from her.

To herself, in that it will give her sweet inward comfort, though her husband should take no notice of her subjection, or misinterpret it, or repay it badly. For she might say as Hezekiah did, “Remember, O #smallcaps[Lord], how I have walked before You in truth and with wholehearted devotion; I have done what was good in Your sight.”#footnote[Isa 38:3.]

That the church seasons all her subjection with sincerity is clear from the fact that she is said to be “all glorious within”#footnote[Ps 45:13.] (there #emph[is no glory within] without sincerity), and that she is often said to seek the one #emph[her soul] loved.#footnote[Song 3:1, 2.] If #emph[her soul] loved him, she was subject to him in sincerity of heart. #emph[Therefore as the church is sincerely subject to Christ, so let wives be to their husbands.]

=== §. 58. #emph[Of wives’ merely outward subjection.]

Contrary to sincerity is pretense, and a merely outward subjection, for show: when a wife even despises her husband in her heart, as Michal did David,#footnote[2 Sam 6:16. Prov 30:20.] and yet puts on a fair face before him, like that adulterous woman who “eats and wipes her mouth and says, ‘I have done nothing wrong.’” Solomon makes it a mark of a wicked wife to flatter with her words. #footnote[Prov 2:16.] Though such a wife should perform all the duties named before, yet they would all be nothing to God if they were done with a double heart and not in singleness of heart. For as many outward imperfections are pardoned by God where sincerity is, so no outward actions are accepted by him, however fair they seem, where there is no sincerity.

=== §. 59. #emph[Of wives’ cheerfulness in every duty.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 4, §. 65, 74.]

#smallcaps[III]. Cheerfulness is more visible than sincerity, and makes subjection the more pleasing not only to God but also to man, who may easily discern it by its effects.

#emph[As for God,] just as he himself does all things willingly and cheerfully, so he expects his children to follow him in this, and so to show themselves his children. “God loves a #footnote[2 Cor 9:7.] cheerful giver”: not only a cheerful giver of alms, but of every duty to God and man.

#emph[As for men,] it also makes them accept any duty much better when they observe that it is done cheerfully. It even enraptured David with joy to see his people offer their gifts #emph[willingly] #footnote[1 Chr 29:9.] to the Lord. When a husband sees his wife perform her duty willingly and cheerfully, it cannot but stir up love in him. This cheerfulness is shown by a ready, quick and speedy performance of her duty. Sarah’s readiness to obey #footnote[Gen 18.] shows that what she did, she did willingly.

That the church subjects herself to Christ in this way is evident from what David says, “Your people shall be willing in the day #footnote[Ps 110:3.] of Your power.” #emph[Therefore as the church is cheerfully subject to Christ, so let wives be to their husbands.]

=== §. 60. #emph[Of wives’ sullen and forced obedience.]

Contrary to this cheerfulness is the sullen disposition of some wives, who will indeed be subject to their husbands and obey, but with such a scowling and sour face, with such pouting and muttering, that their husbands are grieved more by the manner than they can be pleased with the thing itself that their wives do. In this they show themselves like a bad-tempered cow, which, having given a good yield of milk, kicks it all over with her heel; and so they prove the proverb true, #emph[As good not to do it at all as to be never the better for it.] Such subjection is in truth no subjection. It can be neither acceptable to God, nor profitable to their husbands, nor comforting to their own souls.

=== §. 61. #emph[Of wives’ constancy in doing their duty.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 4, §. 72.]

#smallcaps[IIII]. Constancy is a virtue which makes all the rest perfect and sets the crown on them; without it they are all nothing. It is found in those who, after they have begun well, continue to do well to the end, and so reap the fruit of all. It concerns both #emph[continuance] without interruption and #emph[perseverance] without revolting and giving up altogether. So it is not enough to be subject by starts and fits, at one time yielding all good obedience, at another stubborn and rebellious. Nor is it enough to have been a good wife in former times and afterward prove bad. There must be daily going forward and holding on, continually, as long as husband and wife live together. This grace was in her of whom it is said, “She brings him good and not harm all the days of her life.” Such were all the holy wives commended in Scripture. Among other particulars, mention is made of the wife of Phinehas,#footnote[1 Sam 4:21.] who on her deathbed showed the reverent, good respect she had for her husband, though he was a wicked and vile man. This grace the church adds to all her other virtues: in all parts of her subjection she remains constant and faithful to death. By this it comes about that at length she receives the reward of her holy obedience, which is full and perfect communion and fellowship with her spouse Christ Jesus in heaven. Because of her immovable constancy it is said that “the gates of Hades will never prevail against #footnote[Matt 16:18.] her.” #emph[Therefore as the church is constantly subject to Christ, so let wives be to their husbands.]

=== §. 62. #emph[Of wives’ repenting of their former goodness.] #footnote[Contrary, 1. Interruption.]

Contrary to this constancy is, first, #emph[interruption of duty,] a returning to it and a leaving off by turns, like one who is sick with a recurring fever: sometimes well, sometimes ill, now hot, now cold. That #emph[sometimes ceasing] takes away all the worth, grace and glory from the #emph[sometimes doing.] Besides, it is twenty to one that, through the corruption of nature, that changing and alternation of fits will at length cease and end in the worse. It is very likely that Michal was such a one. For at one time she showed herself so full of respect for David that #footnote[1 Sam 19:11, #emph[etc.]] for his sake she incurred the displeasure of the king, her father; at another time she despised him in her heart, and with her tongue #footnote[2 Sam 6:16, 20.] taunted him.

Contrary also to the constancy named above is #emph[apostasy,] #footnote[2\. Apostasy.] that is, a complete abandoning of the former good course, as if a wife repented of her former good beginning. Such a one is she who is said to “abandon the guide of her youth and forget the #footnote[Prov 2:17.] covenant of her God.” For all we read to the contrary, Job’s wife was such a one. And such are many who in their younger years, while their religious parents lived (as Joash#footnote[2 Chr 24:2.] did while good old Jehoiada lived), behaved themselves very well, like good, dutiful wives; but having grown older, have also grown so stubborn and rebellious that it is as if they altogether repented of their former good beginning. This revolt arises sometimes from the evil counsel of wicked companions, and sometimes from their own proud disposition. I may say of these wives’ #emph[subjection,] as the prophet says of the righteousness of those who turn away, #footnote[Ezek 18:24.] #emph[their subjection will not be remembered, but in their rebellion they will die.] Therefore as the church is subject to Christ, let wives be to their husbands.

=== §. 63. #emph[Of the extent of a wife’s obedience.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 4, §. 26.]

The extent of a wife’s subjection (which now remains to be handled) is set down in these general terms (in everything),#footnote[Eph 5:21. #emph[&] 22. Col 3:18.] which are not to be taken so generally as if they admitted no restraint or limitation; for then they would contradict such cautions as these, #emph[in the fear of the Lord, as to the Lord, in the Lord.] For man is so corrupt by nature, and of so perverse a disposition, that he often wills and commands what is contrary to God’s will and commandment. When he does so, that Christian principle laid down by the apostle as a settled case must prevail, “We must obey God rather than men.” #footnote[Acts 5:29.]

#emph[Question.] Why then is this extent laid down in such general terms?

#emph[Answer.] 1. To teach wives that it is not enough for them to obey their husbands in some things, as they themselves think fitting, but in all things whatever in which the husband, by virtue of his superiority and authority, has power to command his wife. So this general extent excludes not God’s will, but the wife’s will. She may do nothing against God’s will; but she must do many things against her own will if her husband requires it of her.

2\. To show that the husband’s authority and power is very large: it has no restraint but God’s contrary command, and if a wife is not sure of that, she must yield to her husband’s will.

=== §. 64. #emph[Of a wife’s striving to bring her judgment into line with her husband’s.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 4, §. 28, 29.]

From that extent I gather these two conclusions:

1\. #emph[A wife must strive to bring her judgment and will into line with her husband’s.]

2\. #emph[Though in her judgment she cannot think what her husband requires to be the most fitting, yet she must yield to it in practice.]

In the former of these, I do not say absolutely that a wife is bound to bring her judgment into line with her husband’s. For he may be deceived in his judgment, and she may see his error; and then, unless her understanding were blinded, she cannot hold to be true what he judges to be so. But I speak of an endeavor (when she has no sure and undeniable grounds to the contrary) to suspect her own judgment when it is contrary to her husband’s, and to think that she may be in error, and so not to be too peremptory and resolute in contradicting her husband’s opinion. This submission, even of her judgment, concerns not only necessary things, for which her husband has an express, definite warrant from Scripture, but also things doubtful, and things neither commanded nor forbidden; for this clause #emph[(in everything)] extends even so far. And the subjection of a wife concerns not her practice only, but her judgment and opinion also. If she can bring her judgment to the lawfulness and fitness of what her husband requires, she will perform it much more cheerfully. To this purpose (as I take it) may be applied that exhortation of the apostle to women, that they “learn in quietness and full subjection.” #footnote[1 Tim 2:11.] Though this is meant chiefly of learning in the church, yet it does not exclude her learning at home from her husband: for in the next words he adds, “I do not permit a woman … to usurp authority over a man, but to be in silence.”

=== §. 65. #emph[Of wives’ overweening opinion of their own wisdom.]

Contrary to this is the presumption of those wives who think themselves wiser than their husbands, and better able to judge matters than they are. I do not deny that a wife may have more understanding than her husband. For some men are very ignorant and dull; and on the other side, some women are well instructed, and have by this attained a great measure of knowledge and discretion. But many, though they have husbands of sufficient and good understanding, wise and discreet men, yet think that what they have once conceived to be true must be so. And such is their obstinacy that they will not be brought to think that they may err, but say that they will never be brought to think otherwise than they do, though all the husbands in the world were of another opinion. They are not much unlike the wise man’s fool, who thinks himself wiser than seven men #footnote[Prov 26:16.] who can give a reason.

=== §. 66. #emph[Of a wife’s yielding to her husband in things she thinks are not the most fitting.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 4, §. 27.]

The latter conclusion, concerning a wife’s yielding in practice to what her husband requires, though she cannot bring her judgment to think as he does about its fitness, #footnote[Si res aliqua occurrat, quae consilio opus habeat, quin ipsa quoque quid censeas dicas minimè prohibeo: sed viri tui sententiam vim maiorem semper obtinere volo. \[If any matter arises that needs counsel, I do not at all forbid you to say what you yourself think; but I want your husband’s opinion always to carry the greater weight.\] Greg. Naz. ad Olymp.] concerns things left free, namely, such as are in their particulars neither commanded nor forbidden by God: as the outward affairs of the house, ordering it, disposing of goods, receiving guests, etc.

#emph[Question.] May she not reason with her husband about such matters as she thinks unfitting, and labor to persuade her husband not to persist in pressing them? Indeed, may she not endeavor to bring her husband to see the unfitness (as she thinks) of what she sees?

#emph[Answer.] With modesty, humility and reverence she may do so, and he ought to listen to her, as the husband of the Shunammite did, 2 Kgs 4:23, 24. But yet, if despite all she can say he persists in his resolution and will have it done, she must yield.

First, her subjection is shown most of all in such cases. In them she plainly shows that what she does, she does out of respect for her husband’s place and power: were it not for that, she would not do it. Other things are not such evident proofs of her subjection to her husband. For if he commands her to do what God has expressly commanded, so that she ought to do it whether her husband commanded it or not, it may be thought that she does it at God’s command, and not at her husband’s. If her husband commands her to do what God has expressly forbidden, then she ought by no means to yield to it. If she does, it may rather be called a joint conspiracy of husband and wife together against God’s will (as Peter said to Sapphira, the wife of Ananias, “How could you#super[+] agree together to test the #footnote[Acts 5:9.] Spirit of the Lord?”) than subjection to the image of God in her husband.

Secondly, her yielding in things neither commanded nor forbidden tends greatly to the peace of the family, as subjects’ yielding to their magistrates in such cases makes greatly for the peace of the commonwealth. For in differences and dissensions one side must yield, or else great injury is likely to follow; and of the two, who should yield but the inferior?

=== §. 67. #emph[Of wives’ making their own will their law.]

Contrary to this is the custom of many wives, who will never do more than they themselves think fitting, however much their husbands require it. Surely they come far short of the apostle’s extent #emph[(in everything),] though in their own eyes they may seem to be very subject. But when wives will be subject no further than their own judgments, wills and desires agree with their husbands’, what can be thought but that they are subject to their own wills rather than to the will of their husbands? Many such wives, from the least difference in judgment and opinion, even in the smallest matters, take occasion to refuse subjection, and think they have warrant enough to do so. From this much contention often arises, the fault of which lies especially on the wife’s neck, though the occasion may arise from the husband. And I think that wives themselves would judge so of similar cases between them and their children.

=== §. 68. #emph[Of care in choosing husbands to whom wives may be subject without grief.]#footnote[See Treatise 2, Part 1, §. 11.]

#emph[Objection.] If the case is so between man and wife, it is not good to marry.

#emph[Answer.] This is no good inference. For all the seeming hardness of a wife’s case lies in the wickedness of a husband who abuses his place and power, and not in the subjection which is required by God. For if a husband carries himself toward his wife as #footnote[Tutum obsequium est, quoties qui dux est, ipse Dei Spiritu ducitur. \[Obedience is safe whenever he who leads is himself led by the Spirit of God.\]] God requires, she will find her yoke easy, and her subjection a great benefit even to herself. Therefore I would urge those who are unmarried, whether maidens or widows, to be very careful in their choice of husbands; and in their choice to regard above all their good qualities and character, in which they bear the image of Christ as well as in their office and authority, so that their wives may be subject to them with joy and comfort, not with grief and anguish. Subjection will prove a vexation when the husband is an ignorant, profane, idolatrous, worldly, wicked man: the wives of such husbands are often brought into many straits. You#super[+] widows and maidens who are free, do not be too free and eager in giving your#super[+] consent to someone you#super[+] do not know. Among other motives, think often of this point of subjection, to which all wives are bound: I say this both of its separate branches and of its extent. After you#super[+] are married, it is vain to think of freedom from subjection. By taking husbands, and giving yourselves#super[+] to be wives, you#super[+] bind yourselves#super[+] to the law of the man as long as he lives. Then, as you#super[+] desire to be accepted by God and to find mercy and comfort from him, you#super[+] must bear this yoke, however heavy and grievous it may seem to be.

=== §. 69. #emph[Of the reasons to move wives to do their duties.]

So much for wives’ duties. The reasons noted by the apostle to urge those duties now follow. They are laid down in these words.

Eph 5:22. #emph[—As to the Lord.]

v. 23. #emph[For the husband is the head of the wife, as Christ is the head of the church; and He is the Savior of the body.]

v. 24. #emph[Therefore, as the church is subject,] etc.

The main ground of all the reasons which the apostle suggests here is taken from the #emph[place] in which God has set a husband. This is first implied by consequence in these words, #emph[as to the Lord,] and then more plainly and directly expressed in these, #emph[the husband is the head of the wife.] The particle set before these words #emph[(for),] being a #emph[causal conjunction,] shows that they are set down here as a reason. This reason is first set forth under a metaphor #emph[(head),] and then amplified by the resemblance which a husband has in this to Christ #emph[(as Christ, etc.).] This resemblance is further commended by the power and benefit that proceed from the headship of Christ in the strict sense, and of a husband also by consequence, in these words #emph[(and He is the Savior of the body).] From a husband’s resemblance to Christ he infers that a wife should resemble the church, and so concludes, #emph[Therefore, as the church is subject to Christ, so let wives be to their husbands.]

From the ground named above for a wife’s subjection, its separate amplifications, and the inference drawn from it, five separate and distinct reasons may be gathered to urge a wife’s subjection to her husband.

The first is taken from a husband’s #emph[place:] he stands in the Lord’s place to his wife #emph[(as to the Lord).]

The second from his #emph[office:] he is a #emph[head] to his wife.

The third from the #emph[image] he bears, or from the #emph[resemblance] between him and Christ #emph[(as Christ, etc.)]

The fourth from the #emph[benefit] that his wife receives from him #emph[(He is the Savior, etc.)]

The fifth from the #emph[example] and pattern of the church #emph[(as the church is subject, etc.)]

=== §. 70. #emph[Of a husband’s place.]

The #emph[place] in which God has set a husband, as it serves #footnote[1\. #emph[Reason.]] to direct a wife in the #emph[manner] of her subjection, of which I have spoken #footnote[§. 51.] before, so it also serves to move a wife to yield such subjection as is required. This will clearly appear from these two conclusions which follow from it.

1\. #emph[A wife, by subjecting herself to her husband, is in this subject to Christ.]

2\. #emph[A wife, by refusing to be subject to her husband, in this refuses to be subject to Christ.]

That these two conclusions are rightly and justly gathered from the ground named above, I prove by like conclusions which the Holy Spirit infers from a like ground. It is evident that Christ Jesus, even incarnate and made flesh, was in the place of his Father. That is why Christ said to Philip, who desired to see the Father, “Anyone who has seen Me has seen the Father.”#footnote[John 14:9. Matt 10:40. John 5:23.] Now mark what Christ infers from this, both on the one side (“he who receives Me receives the One who sent Me”) and on the other (“whoever does not honor the Son does not honor the Father who sent Him”). It is also evident that ministers of the gospel stand in the place of Christ: for so the apostle says of himself and other ministers, “We are ambassadors for Christ, as though God were making His appeal through us. We implore you#super[+] in Christ’s place, etc.” Now mark again the conclusions Christ infers from this: on the one side, “Whoever listens to #footnote[Luke 10:16.] you#super[+] listens to Me,” and on the other, “whoever rejects you#super[+] rejects Me.” It was on this ground that God said to Samuel, concerning the people who rejected his government, “It is not you they have rejected, but they have rejected Me.” #footnote[1 Sam 8:7.]

To apply this reason: I hope such wives as live under the gospel have so much religion and piety in them as to acknowledge that it well becomes them to be subject to the Lord Christ Jesus. Here, then, learn one special and principal part of subjection to Christ, which is to be subject to your#super[+] husbands. In this way you#super[+] will show yourselves#super[+] to be the wives of the Lord Christ, as the apostle says of obedient servants that they are “servants of God.” #footnote[1 Pet 2:16.]

Again, I hope none are so void of all religion and piety as to refuse to be subject to Christ. Here, then, take notice that if you#super[+] willfully refuse to be subject to your#super[+] husbands, you#super[+] willfully refuse to be subject to Christ. On this ground I may fitly apply to wives what the apostle says of subjects: #emph[whoever resists the power and authority of a husband] #footnote[Rom 13:2.] #emph[resists the ordinance of God, and those who resist will bring judgment on themselves.]

This first motive is a strong motive. If wives duly considered it, they would be subject more readily and cheerfully than many are. They would not think so lightly of their husband’s place, nor speak so reproachfully against God’s ministers, who plainly declare their duty to them, as many do.

=== §. 71. #emph[Of a husband’s office.]

The second reason, like this one, is taken from a husband’s #footnote[2\. #emph[Reason.] 1 Cor 11:3.] office: #emph[he is the wife’s head.] This is also urged for this very purpose in other places.

This metaphor shows that he is to his wife as the head of a natural body, both more #emph[eminent] in place and more #emph[excellent] in dignity; and by virtue of both he is a ruler and governor of his wife. Nature teaches us that this is true of the head of a natural body; and the apostle, by calling a husband #emph[a head,] teaches us that it is as true of a husband. From this it follows that it agrees with common equity and with the light of nature that the wife should be subject to her husband. The apostle urges this argument in plain terms in another place, saying, “Does not nature itself teach you#super[+], etc.?” #footnote[1 Cor 11:14.]

Go therefore, O wives, to the school of nature; look at the outward parts and members of your#super[+] bodies. Do they desire to be above the head? Are they reluctant to be subject to the head? Then let your#super[+] soul learn from your#super[+] body. Would it not be monstrous for the #emph[side] to be raised above the #emph[head?] If the body were not subject to the head, would not destruction fall on head, body and all its parts? Just as monstrous, and much more monstrous, is it for a wife to be above her husband; and as great, yes, and greater, disturbance and ruin would fall on that family. The order which God has set in it would be completely overthrown by this, and those who overthrow it would show themselves opponents of God’s wisdom in establishing order. This reason drawn from nature has force to move even pagans and savages to yield subjection; how much more Christian wives, since it also agrees with God’s word and is confirmed by it!

=== §. 72. #emph[Of the resemblance between Christ and a husband.]

The third reason, taken from a husband’s #emph[resemblance] to #footnote[3\. #emph[Reason.]] Christ in this, adds an edge to the former reason: in being a head, he is like Christ. So there is a kind of fellowship and partnership between Christ and a husband: they are brethren in office, like two kings of separate realms.

#emph[Objection.] There is no equality between Christ, the Lord from heaven, and an earthly husband: the disparity between them is infinite.

#emph[Answer.] Yet there may be likeness, resemblance and fellowship: inequality is no hindrance to these. Two kings may be more different in rank than a subject and a king, and yet those two kings are brethren and fellows in office. There may be a resemblance where there is no parity, and a likeness where there is no equality. The glorious, bright sun in the sky and a dim candle in a house have a kind of fellowship and the same office, which is to give light; yet there is no equality between them. So, then, a husband resembles not only the head of a natural body, but also the glorious image of Christ, and is that to his wife which Christ is to his church.

To apply this point, mark how two positions (worth noting) arise from it.

1\. #emph[Subjection is due to a husband as well as to Christ.] #footnote[Subjection due to a husband as well as to Christ.]

I do not say #emph[as great,] because of the difference in glory, but #emph[as well,] because of the likeness in office. A constable (though a poor, lowly man) must be obeyed as well as a high sheriff; a beggar’s child must obey his father as well as a king’s child. Therefore wives who are not subject wrong their husbands, just as those who are not subject to Christ wrong him.

2\. #emph[Those who by their subjection maintain the honor of their husband’s place maintain by this the honor of Christ’s place;] and again, by the rule of contraries, #emph[those who by refusing to be subject impair the honor of their husband’s place impair by this the honor of Christ’s place.]

The obedience of a poor man’s child or servant vindicates the obedience which kings’ children and servants owe their father and sovereign; and so, on the contrary, disobedience in lowly persons dishonors the place of great ones.

The argument of Memucan, drawn from the greater to the #footnote[Esth 1:16.] less (in these words, “Queen Vashti has wronged not only the king, but all the princes and the peoples”), may be applied from the less to the greater. Disobedient wives wrong not only their own particular husbands, but also all heads, even Christ the head of the church.

If a natural body and the church were pliable, and could be seduced and drawn to act presumptuously and rebel against their heads, the bad example of wives would be enough to move them to it; for, as far as lies in them, they seduce them by their example.

From the last positions named above (namely, that #emph[the obedience of a good wife maintains the honor of Christ’s place,] and on the contrary side that #emph[the disobedience of a bad wife impairs its honor]) I may justly infer two other conclusions.

1\. That #emph[Christ will surely reward the good subjection of] #footnote[Christ will reward the good subjection, and avenge the rebellion, of wives.] #emph[good wives:] for he has said (and what he has said he can and will perform), “I will honor those who honor Me.”

2\. #emph[That he will severely avenge the rebellion of evil wives:] for again he has said, “Those who despise Me will be despised.”

We know that fellows in office are ready to stand up for the #footnote[1 Sam 2:30.] good name of one another’s place, and to maintain its honor; and that not without good reason, for by this they maintain their own honor and good name.

Therefore, as good wives may well expect a reward at Christ’s hands, however their husbands regard their obedience, whether well or badly (a great encouragement for wives to perform their duties, however bad their husbands are), so evil wives have just cause to fear vengeance at Christ’s hand, however their husbands bear with them.

Those who duly weigh this reason, taken from the resemblance between Christ and the church, cannot but hold it to be a motive of great importance.

=== §. 73. #emph[Of the benefit which a wife has from a husband.]

The fourth reason, taken from the #emph[benefit] which a wife receives #footnote[4\. #emph[Reason.]] from her husband, presses the point in hand still further. Though Christ is in the strict sense the #emph[Savior of the body,] yet even in this a husband bears a resemblance to Christ, and is in a manner a #emph[savior.] For by virtue of his place and office he is, on the one side, her #emph[protector,] to defend her from harm and preserve her from danger; and on the other side, a #emph[provider] of all needful and necessary things for her. In this respect she is taken from her parents and relatives and wholly committed #footnote[Gen 31:14.] to him (as Jacob’s wives said, “Do we have any portion or inheritance left in our father’s house?”). Yes, she herself and all she has are given to him; and he in turn imparts whatever he has for her good and for her use. David compares a wife to #footnote[Ps 128:3.] a #emph[vine] in relation to her husband, suggesting by this that she is raised by him to the height of honor she has, as a vine is raised by the tree or frame near which it is planted. By his honor she is dignified; by his wealth she is enriched. Under God he is all in all to her. In the family he is a #emph[king] to govern and aid her, a #emph[priest] to pray with her and for her, a #emph[prophet] to teach and instruct her. As the head is placed in the highest place over the body, and understanding is placed in it, to govern, direct, protect and in every way seek the good of the body; and as Christ is united to the church as a spouse, and made her head, so that she might be saved, maintained and provided for by him: so for this end a husband was placed in his place of superiority, and his authority was committed to him, to be a #emph[savior] of his wife. Therefore, if none of the former motives prevails with wives and moves them to be subject to their husbands, yet this one ought to.

For from this reason flow these two conclusions.

+ #emph[The subjection required of a wife is for her own good.]
+ #emph[In refusing to obey, she shows herself both ungrateful to her husband and injurious to herself.]

That her subjection is for her own good is evident from this #footnote[A wife’s subjection tends to her own good.] end for which a husband is made a head: to be a #emph[savior,] not to puff him up, or to make him domineer and tyrannize over his wife. So if she is subject to him, she may reap much good from him. As the church is wisely governed and safely protected by subjecting herself to her head, Christ Jesus, and as the body shares in much good and is preserved from much evil by subjecting itself to the head, so if a wife is subject to her husband she will fare much the better for it: all the ease, profit and benefit of it will be hers. If, therefore, she cares for her own good, this is a way and means ordained by God for this end; let her seek it in this.

If, despite this, she refuses to be subject to her husband, does she not (as we say) stand in her own light? Being by her sex the weaker, and the less able to help herself, if she rejects this good help which God has provided for her, is she not most injurious to her own self? And considering #footnote[Gratitude requires a wife to be subject.] the care and pains her husband undergoes for her sake, is it not most unnatural and monstrous ingratitude inwardly to despise, or outwardly to scorn, such a head? No better proof of a grateful heart can be given by a wife to her husband than cheerful and ready subjection, and no greater ingratitude can be shown than rebellion and disdain. Now among vices ingratitude is one of the most odious to God and man: so, both to avoid the black spot of ingratitude and to win the name of gratefulness, wives ought to be subject.

=== §. 74. #emph[Of the example of the church set before wives.]

The last reason, taken from the #emph[example] of the church, is also #footnote[5\. #emph[Reason.]] of good force to persuade wives to #emph[subjection.] Example prevails more with many than precept. If any example may have force, then this one most of all. For it is not the example of one only, but of many; not of many ignorant and wicked persons, but of understanding, wise, holy and righteous persons, even all the saints that ever were, are, or will be. For the church includes all under it, even that whole society of saints who are chosen by God in his eternal counsel, redeemed by Christ with his precious blood, and effectually called by the gospel of salvation, God’s Spirit working inwardly and powerfully on them; not excepting those very souls of just and perfect men now triumphing in heaven. Note how this church is described in vv. 26 and 27. Let this example therefore be thought of often: no one will ever regret following it. For it treads the only right path to eternal glory, to which those who follow it will surely come.

But to show the force of this reason a little more distinctly, note these two conclusions that follow from it.

1\. #emph[Wives are as much bound to be subject to their husbands as the church is to Christ.] Otherwise why should this example be set before them in this way and pressed on them? Why are husbands set in Christ’s place and likened to him?

2\. #emph[A wife’s subjection to her husband, corresponding to the church’s subjection to Christ, is evidence that she is of the church,] guided by the same Spirit that the church is. For it cannot be performed by the power of nature; it is a supernatural work, and so an evidence of the Spirit.

Therefore, O Christian wives, as your#super[+] husbands resemble Christ by their place, so you#super[+] resemble the church by your#super[+] practice. Of the two, this is the more commendable: for that is a dignity, this a virtue; but true virtue is much more glorious than any dignity can be.

When these reasons are well weighed, and the force of them all joined together, they cannot but work on the most stubborn stomach there is. Therefore, if this point of subjection seems too bitter a pill to be well digested, let it be sweetened with the syrup of these reasons, and it will be swallowed much better, and work to all the better effect.
